// PostsList One page of a thread's posts: positions [(page-1)*limit, page*limit), in position order. `cursor` (from the previous page) takes precedence over `page`. Posts the viewer may not see are left out, so a page can hold fewer than `limit` posts (moderators see every state; authors also see their own unapproved posts). `nextCursor` is null after the last page. `afterPosition` (the highest position the client holds) starts the page at the position after it, so a client can poll for replies: the answer is empty when there is nothing new. A `cursor` takes precedence over it, and it over `page`. With `afterPosition` the answer also carries `afterPostId`, the id of the post now at that position (null when there is none, or when the viewer may not see it, as with the items). Merging threads into this one renumbers positions, so the rule for a poller is: when `afterPostId` is not the id of the newest post it holds, drop what it holds and reload the thread from the start, ignoring the items of that answer; otherwise append the items. For a merge the check is exact: posts merged in below the held ones change the id at that position, while posts merged in above it and replies do not. A split does not renumber the posts that stay; it changes the id at that position only when the held newest post itself is split out, and a post that turns hidden for the viewer gives null. A poll does not show edits, removals or splits of other posts the client already holds, nor a post approved after newer ones were posted (it keeps its old position): those show on the next reload. While a merge into the thread is running, each answer costs a pass over the posts of the thread and its sources instead of an index read.
